1.1.text:
Article 9

(1) The Regional Government in an effort to prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children shall carry out empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall carry out efforts as follows:

a. improving the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income improvement and social services;
c. creating job opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and awareness toward the prevention of protection for women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system; and
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children victims of violence.

Article 4

The implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence aims to:

a. prevent all forms of violent acts including human trafficking;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect, provide a sense of security for victims of violence; d. provide services to victims of violence; and
e. provide comprehensive recovery to victims.

Article 6

(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a, include:

a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
b. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection for women and children who are victims of violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection for women and children who are victims of violence;
d. allocating budget for the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
e. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.
(2) In carrying out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent shall establish action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in one Regional Action Plan as the basis for SKPD in implementing the protection of women and children victims of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 10

(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 9, shall be implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:

a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. peace and order.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 20

(1) The Regional Government shall conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence. (2) Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. guidelines and standards of fulfillment;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and
e. evaluation.
(3) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is aimed at realizing the objectives of protecting women and children victims of violence in accordance with minimum service standards.

Article 2

The implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:

a. respect for human rights;
b. gender equality and justice;
c. non-discrimination;
d. the best interests of the victim; and
e. respect for the rights of women and children.

Article 11

(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence, are as follows:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal; and
f. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Regional Government and implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.In the implementation of services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may cooperate with Government agencies, other Regional Governments and the community.
(3) Further provisions regarding procedures for services for handling the protection of women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 16

Women and children victims of violence are entitled to the following rights:

a. right to be respected for their dignity and worth as human beings; b. right to physical and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. right to make their own decisions;
d. right to receive information;
e. right to confidentiality;
f. right to social rehabilitation;
g. right to compensation;
h. right to complaint handling;
i. right of victims and their families to receive facilitation in the judicial process; and
j. right to assistance.

Article 17

Children who become victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 16, also receive special rights, as follows:

a. right to respect and full enjoyment for survival, growth and development;
b. right to basic services;
c. right to equal protection;
d. right to freedom from various stigmas; and
e. right to freedom.

Article 5

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to women and children victims of violence are the shared responsibility of:

a. Regional Government;
b. Community;
c. Family; and
d. Parents.

